To find the kth largest element in a given list of numbers without using any built-in sorting functions or libraries, we can use the QuickSelect algorithm.

The QuickSelect algorithm is similar to the QuickSort algorithm but avoids sorting the entire list. It partitions the list around a pivot element and recursively applies the partitioning process to only one side of the pivot, depending on the desired position of the kth largest element. This process is repeated until the kth largest element is found.

Here is the Python code to implement the QuickSelect algorithm:

```python
def partition(arr, low, high):
    pivot = arr[low]
    i = low + 1
    j = high

    while True:
        while i <= j and arr[i] < pivot:
            i += 1
        while i <= j and arr[j] >= pivot:
            j -= 1
        if i <= j:
            arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]
        else:
            break

    arr[low], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[low]

    return j

def quickSelect(arr, low, high, k):
    pivotIndex = partition(arr, low, high)
    if pivotIndex == k - 1:
        return arr[pivotIndex]
    elif pivotIndex > k - 1:
        return quickSelect(arr, low, pivotIndex - 1, k)
    else:
        return quickSelect(arr, pivotIndex + 1, high, k)

def findKthLargest(arr, k):
    return quickSelect(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1, k)
```

