        System.out.println("Largest prime number: " + largestPrime);

        int primeCount = countPrimes(start, end);
        System.out.println("Number of prime numbers found: " + primeCount);
    }

    // Function to check if a number is prime
    public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
            if (num % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    // Function to check if a number is palindrome
    public static boolean isPalindrome(int num) {
        int original = num;
        int reverse = 0;

        while (num != 0) {
            int remainder = num % 10;
            reverse = reverse * 10 + remainder;
            num /= 10;
        }

        return original == reverse;
    }

    // Function to find the largest prime number within a range, excluding palindromes
    public static int findLargestPrime(int start, int end) {
        int largestPrime = -1;

        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i) && !isPalindrome(i)) {
                largestPrime = i;
            }
        }

        return largestPrime;
    }

    // Function to count the number of prime numbers within a range, excluding palindromes
    public static int countPrimes(int start, int end) {
        int primeCount = 0;

        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i) && !isPalindrome(i)) {
                primeCount++;
            }
        }

        return primeCount;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `isPrime` function checks if a given number is prime by iterating from 2 to the square root of the number and checking for any divisors. The `isPalindrome` function checks if a number is a palindrome by reversing its digits and comparing it with the original number.

The `findLargestPrime` function iterates over the range of numbers and checks if each number is prime and not a palindrome. If a number satisfies both conditions, it is considered as a potential largest prime number. The function keeps track of the largest prime number found so far and returns it.

The `countPrimes` function is similar to `findLargestPrime`, but it increments a counter whenever a prime number that is not a palindrome is found within the range. The counter is returned as the result.

