Here is a Java program that prompts the user to enter a positive integer n and calculates the sum of the first n prime numbers:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class SumOfFirstNPrimes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = 0;
        boolean validInput = false;

        while (!validInput) {
            System.out.print("Enter a positive integer n: ");

            try {
                n = Integer.parseInt(input.nextLine());

                if (n > 0) {
                    validInput = true;
                } else {
                    System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a positive integer.");
                }
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a positive integer.");
            }
        }

        int count = 0;
        int sum = 0;
        int number = 2;

        while (count < n) {
            if (isPrime(number)) {
                sum += number;
                count++;
            }
            number++;
        }

        System.out.println("Sum of the first " + n + " prime numbers is: " + sum);
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num < 2) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
            if (num % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

In this program, we use a while loop to prompt the user for a positive integer n until a valid input is provided. The input is validated using a try-catch block and the `Integer.parseInt()` method to convert the input string to an integer.

We then use another while loop to find the first n prime numbers. The `isPrime()` method is called to check if a number is prime. If it is prime, we add it to the sum and increment the count. The while loop continues until the count reaches n.

Finally, we print the sum of the first n prime numbers.

