Here is one possible implementation of the `findLargestPrime` function:

```javascript
function isPrime(num) {
  if (num <= 1) {
    return false;
  }

  for (let i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
    if (num % i === 0) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  return true;
}

function findLargestPrime(arr) {
  if (arr.length === 0) {
    return null;
  }

  let largestPrime = null;

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const num = arr[i];

    if (isPrime(num) && (largestPrime === null || num > largestPrime)) {
      largestPrime = num;
    }
  }

  return largestPrime;
}

// Example usage:
const arr = [5, 10, 2, 7, 3];
const largestPrime = findLargestPrime(arr);
console.log(largestPrime); // Output: 7
```

In this implementation, we first define a helper function `isPrime` that checks if a number is prime. This function uses a basic prime-checking algorithm that iterates from 2 to the square root of the number, checking if any of those numbers divide the number evenly.

The `findLargestPrime` function then iterates through the array, checking if each element is prime and if it is larger than the current largest prime found so far. If both conditions are true, we update the `largestPrime` variable. At the end, we return the `largestPrime` found.

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements in the array, as we iterate through each element once.

