Here's an implementation of a stack class in C# that satisfies all the mentioned functionalities:

```csharp
using System;

public class Stack<T>
{
    private T[] items;
    private int top;

    public Stack(int size)
    {
        items = new T[size];
        top = -1;
    }

    public void Push(T item)
    {
        if (top == items.Length - 1)
        {
            throw new StackOverflowException("Stack is full");
        }

        items[++top] = item;
    }

    public T Pop()
    {
        if (IsEmpty())
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Stack is empty");
        }

        return items[top--];
    }

    public T Peek()
    {
        if (IsEmpty())
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Stack is empty");
        }

        return items[top];
    }

    public bool IsEmpty()
    {
        return top == -1;
    }

    public int Count()
    {
        return top + 1;
    }
}
```

To handle the error appropriately when performing an operation on an empty stack, we use exception handling. In this case, the code throws an `InvalidOperationException` with a descriptive error message stating that the stack is empty. This allows the caller to catch the exception and handle it as necessary.

