```rust
use rustc_middle::ty::{Ty, TyCtxt, AdtDef, subst::SubstsRef};
use rustc_middle::ty::layout::TyLayout;
use rustc_data_structures::fx::FxHashSet;

fn analyze_adt(ty: Ty<'_>, tcx: TyCtxt<'_>, visited_types: &mut FxHashSet<Ty<'_>>) -> (bool, bool) {
    if visited_types.contains(&ty) {
        return (false, false);
    }
    visited_types.insert(ty);

    match ty.kind() {
        ty::Adt(adt_def, substs) => {
            let is_root = adt_def.did.is_local();
            let is_fat = adt_def.variants.iter().any(|variant| {
                let crate_name = tcx.crate_name(variant.def_id.krate).as_str();
                let variant_name = variant.ident.name.as_str();
                crate_name == "bronze" && variant_name == "GcRef"
            });

            (is_root, is_fat)
        }
        _ => (false, false)
    }
}
```

In the solution, we first check if the type `ty` has already been visited to avoid infinite recursion. Then, we match the type kind to determine if it is an ADT. If it is, we check if the ADT is a root type by inspecting its definition. We also check if any of its variants match the criteria for being a "fat" type. If the type is not an ADT, we return `(false, false)`.

The bank conflict issue arises from the way shared memory is set up in CUDA. The shared memory in CUDA is divided into different memory banks and each of these banks can be accessed concurrently. However, a bank conflict occurs when more than one thread of a warp tries to access the same memory bank at the same time. 

The issue with the interleaved addressing approach is that it can lead to bank conflicts depending on the stride. If the stride divides the number of banks, every thread in a half-warp will access the same bank, leading to a bank conflict.

For example, in CUDA devices with compute capability 3.x, there are 32 banks of shared memory, and when the stride is a multiple of 32 (like 32, 64, 96 etc), each thread in a half-warp (16 threads) will access the same bank. This leads to a bank conflict, causing serialization of memory accesses and hence reduction in performance.

Here's a simplified example of how bank conflicts occur:

```cpp
__global__ void reduce(int *input, int *results, int N) {
    extern __shared__ int sdata[];

    int tid = threadIdx.x;
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;

    // Load shared mem from global mem
    sdata[tid] = input[i];
    __syncthreads();

    // Interleaved addressing reduction
    for(int s = 1; s < blockDim.x; s *= 2) {
        if(tid % (2*s) == 0) {
            sdata[tid] += sdata[tid + s];
        }
        __syncthreads();
    }

    // write result for block to global mem
    if(tid == 0) {
        results[blockIdx.x] = sdata[0];
    }
}
```

In this code, when `s` is a multiple of 32, all threads in a half-warp will access the same bank, leading to a bank conflict.

