Below is a Java Program demonstrating Circular LinkedList and operations like Clockwise and Counter-Clockwise Rotation.

```java

public class CircularLinkedList {
    class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;
        Node(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    Node last;
    private int capacity, size = 0;

    public CircularLinkedList(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    // Adding a new node
    public boolean add(int value) {
        Node newNode = new Node(value);
        if (size >= capacity)
            return false;
        if (last == null) {
            last = newNode;
            newNode.next = newNode;
        } else {
            newNode.next = last.next;
            last.next = newNode;
            last = newNode;
        }
        size++;
        return true;
    }

    // Rotate list Clockwise
    public void rotateClockwise(int n) {
        if (last == null)
            return;
        n = n % size;
        while (n-- > 0)
            last = last.next;
    }

    // Rotate list Counter-Clockwise
    public void rotateCounterClockwise(int n) {
        if (last == null)
            return;
        n = n % size;
        Node currNode = last;
        while (n-- > 0) {
            currNode = currNode.next;
        }
        last = currNode;
    }

    // Display Linked List
    public void display() {
        if (last == null)
            return;
        Node first = last.next;
        while (first != last) {
            System.out.print(first.value + " -> ");
            first = first.next;
        }
        System.out.println(first.value);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CircularLinkedList circularLinkedList = new CircularLinkedList(5);
        circularLinkedList.add(1);
        circularLinkedList.add(2);
        circularLinkedList.add(3);
        circularLinkedList.add(4);
        circularLinkedList.add(5);
        System.out.println("Original List: ");
        circularLinkedList.display();
        circularLinkedList.rotateClockwise(2);
        System.out.println("List after 2 clockwise rotations: ");
        circularLinkedList.display();
        circularLinkedList.rotateCounterClockwise(2);
        System.out.println("List after 2 counter-clockwise rotations (should match original): ");
        circularLinkedList.display();
    }
}
```

In this program, we define a circular linked list `CircularLinkedList`, where each `Node` consists of an integer value and a pointer to the next node. Rotation is done by changing the last node since it's efficient in a circular linked list. Nodes are added until they reach capacity, avoid overflowing the list.

`rotateClockwise` and `rotateCounterClockwise` methods have a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of rotations. The `add` method has a time complexity of O(1) as it only alters pointers, which is a constant time operation. Note that the actual rotation is done in constant time, but the time complexity is O(n) due to the while loop running 'n' times. 

