function formatTime(seconds) {
  const absSeconds = Math.abs(seconds);
  const days = Math.floor(absSeconds / (24 * 60 * 60));
  const remainingSeconds = absSeconds % (24 * 60 * 60);
  const hours = Math.floor(remainingSeconds / (60 * 60));
  const remainingSeconds2 = remainingSeconds % (60 * 60);
  const minutes = Math.floor(remainingSeconds2 / 60);
  const remainingSeconds3 = remainingSeconds2 % 60;
  const sign = seconds < 0 ? "-" : "";

  let formattedTime = "";
  if (days > 0) {
    formattedTime += days.toString().padStart(2, "0") + ":";
  }
  formattedTime += hours.toString().padStart(2, "0") + ":";
  formattedTime += minutes.toString().padStart(2, "0") + ":";
  formattedTime += remainingSeconds3.toString().padStart(2, "0");

  return sign + formattedTime;
}
```

The function first calculates the number of days by dividing the absolute value of the input by the number of seconds in a day (24 * 60 * 60). The remaining seconds after accounting for days are then calculated. The function then calculates the number of hours, minutes, and seconds from the remaining seconds using similar calculations. The sign of the input is also determined to add a minus sign to the formatted time if necessary.

The formatted time is constructed by adding the appropriate padding to each component (days, hours, minutes, and seconds) using the `padStart` method to ensure they are always two digits long. The formatted time is then returned, with the sign added at the beginning if necessary.

This solution has a time complexity of O(1) because it performs a fixed number of arithmetic operations and string manipulations regardless of the size of the input.

