The problem you're facing is not uncommon when working with Iterative Closest Point (ICP) algorithms or similar point cloud matching tasks. To evaluate the quality of the pose given by the ICP, you might want to consider a consistent quantitative measure. 

One such measure could be the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) between corresponding points after transformation. This would give you a numerical value representing the average distance error per point. The lower the RMSE, the better the pose.

Here's a simple code snippet in C++ that calculates the RMSE:

```cpp
#include <cmath>
#include <vector>

// Assuming that modelPoints and dataPoints are std::vector of Eigen::Vector3d
double calculateRMSE(const std::vector<Eigen::Vector3d>& modelPoints, 
                     const std::vector<Eigen::Vector3d>& dataPoints, 
                     const Eigen::Matrix4d& transform) {
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < modelPoints.size(); i++) {
        Eigen::Vector3d transformedDataPoint = transform * dataPoints[i]; // Apply the transformation
        sum += (modelPoints[i] - transformedDataPoint).squaredNorm(); // Add square of Euclidean distance
    }
    return std::sqrt(sum / modelPoints.size()); // RMSE
}
```

This way you would get a more consistent measure of how well your ICP performs. However, it's important to remember that this method doesn't take into account outliers, which can significantly affect the RMSE. If your data has many outliers, you might want to use a robust statistical measure like the Median Absolute Deviation (MAD) instead of RMSE.

