```java
public static int searchRotatedArray(int[] nums, int target) {
    if (nums == null || nums.length == 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    int start = 0;
    int end = nums.length - 1;

    while (start <= end) {
        int middle = (start + end) / 2;

        if (nums[middle] == target) {
            return middle;
        }

        if (nums[middle] >= nums[start]) {
            if (target >= nums[start] && target < nums[middle]) {
                end = middle - 1;
            } else {
                start = middle + 1;
            }
        } else {
            if (target > nums[middle] && target <= nums[end]) {
                start = middle + 1;
            } else {
                end = middle - 1;
            }
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

This code follows the steps mentioned earlier. It first checks for the edge cases where the array is empty or has only one element. Then, it initializes the start and end variables to the first and last indices of the array, respectively.

Next, it enters a while loop that continues as long as the start index is less than or equal to the end index. Within the while loop, it calculates the middle index using the formula (start + end) / 2. It checks if the middle element is equal to the target element and returns the index if they are equal.

If the middle element is greater than or equal to the first element of the array, it means that the pivot point is to the right of the middle element. In this case, it updates the start variable to be the next index of the middle element. Otherwise, if the middle element is less than the first element of the array, it means that the pivot point is to the left of the middle element. In this case, it updates the end variable to be the previous index of the middle element.

If none of the above conditions are met, it means that the array is not rotated, and it can perform a regular binary search to find the target element.

If the target element is not found after the while loop, it returns -1.

Note: This code assumes that the input array does not contain duplicate elements. If there are duplicate elements, the code may return the index of a different occurrence of the target element.

