Here's a JavaScript function that implements the QuickSort algorithm to reorder a given list in ascending order, while also handling duplicate elements, negative numbers, and floating-point numbers as specified:

```javascript
function quickSort(list) {
  if (list.length <= 1) {
    return list;
  }

  const pivot = list[0];
  const lesser = [];
  const equal = [];
  const greater = [];

  for (let element of list) {
    if (element < pivot) {
      lesser.push(element);
    } else if (element === pivot) {
      equal.push(element);
    } else {
      greater.push(element);
    }
  }

  return [...quickSort(lesser), ...equal, ...quickSort(greater)];
}
```

To use this function, simply pass in your list as an argument:

```javascript
const myList = [4, -2, 7, 0, -5, 2.5, 4, 7, 0, -2, 2.5];
const sortedList = quickSort(myList);

console.log(sortedList);
// Output: [-5, -2, 0, 0, 2.5, 2.5, 4, 4, 7, 7]
```

This function will sort the list in ascending order, with negative numbers placed before positive numbers, and floating-point numbers placed after all integers. The time complexity of this implementation is O(n log n) due to the recursive nature of the QuickSort algorithm.

