Both languages are correct in their implementation of the modulo operation. They just follow different conventions. 

In Python, the sign of the result is determined by the divisor (i.e., the right operand). If the divisor is positive, the result is positive or zero, and if the divisor is negative, the result is negative or zero. This is often referred to as "floored division".

On the other hand, C follows the convention of "truncated division" where the remainder always has the same sign as the dividend (i.e., the left operand). 

If you want to achieve Python's modulo behavior in C, you can use the following function:

```c
int python_modulo(int dividend, int divisor) {
    int remainder = dividend % divisor;
    if (remainder < 0)
