Increase the complexity further by introducing a priority queue functionality to the bounded buffer. Modify the code to implement a bounded buffer with multiple producers and consumers using mutex locks, condition variables, and a priority queue.

```cpp
#include <pthread.h>
#include <queue>

#define BUFFER_SIZE 10
#define NUM_PRODUCERS 3
#define NUM_CONSUMERS 2

struct QueueItem {
    int data;
    int priority;
};

struct ComparePriority {
    bool operator()(const QueueItem& item1, const QueueItem& item2) {
        return item1.priority < item2.priority;
    }
};

std::priority_queue<QueueItem, std::vector<QueueItem>, ComparePriority> buffer;
int count = 0;

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t empty = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;

void* producer_func(void* arg) {
    int data = *(int*)arg;
    int priority = *(int*)arg % NUM_PRODUCERS; // Assign priority based on producer ID
    while (1) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
        while (count == BUFFER_SIZE) {
            pthread_cond_wait(&empty, &mutex);
        }

        QueueItem item;
        item.data = data;
        item.priority = priority;

        buffer.push(item);
        count++;

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

        data += NUM_PRODUCERS; // Increase data by NUM_PRODUCERS to avoid duplicates
        priority = (priority + 1) % NUM_PRODUCERS; // Update priority

        pthread_cond_signal(&empty);
    }
    return NULL;
}

void* consumer_func(void* arg) {
    while (1) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
        while (count == 0) {
            pthread_cond_wait(&empty, &mutex);
        }

        QueueItem item = buffer.top();
        buffer.pop();
        count--;

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

        // Process the data

        printf("Consumed by Thread %d: %d (Priority: %d)\n", *(int*)arg, item.data, item.priority);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t producer_threads[NUM_PRODUCERS], consumer_threads[NUM_CONSUMERS];
    int producer_ids[NUM_PRODUCERS], consumer_ids[NUM_CONSUMERS];

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_PRODUCERS; i++) {
        producer_ids[i] = i;
        pthread_create(&producer_threads[i], NULL, producer_func, (void*)&producer_ids[i]);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_CONSUMERS; i++) {
        consumer_ids[i] = i;
        pthread_create(&consumer_threads[i], NULL, consumer_func, (void*)&consumer_ids[i]);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_PRODUCERS; i++) {
        pthread_join(producer_threads[i], NULL);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_CONSUMERS; i++) {
        pthread_join(consumer_threads[i], NULL);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this further modified test, the code now implements a bounded buffer with multiple producers and consumers, along with a priority queue functionality. The `producer_func` function is executed by multiple producer threads, each generating data and adding it to the priority queue. The `consumer_func` function is executed by multiple consumer threads, each consuming data from the priority queue.

The priority queue is implemented using a `std::priority_queue` container, which stores `QueueItem` objects. Each `QueueItem` consists of the generated data and a priority value, which is determined based on the producer ID. The `ComparePriority` struct defines the comparison operator for the priority queue, ensuring that items with higher priority are consumed first.

The producer threads assign a unique priority to each produced item based on the producer ID. The consumer threads consume the items from the priority queue in the order of their priorities.

The `consumer_func` function also includes the priority value in the `printf` statement to track the priority of each consumed item.

Overall, this further modified test introduces the complexity of managing a priority queue along with multiple producers and consumers, requiring a deeper understanding of synchronization mechanisms and priority-based data processing.

