The observed performance difference when using `cudaMallocManaged` between the Pascal and Maxwell GPUs is likely because of the difference in the architecture of these two GPUs. `cudaMallocManaged` is a feature that was introduced in CUDA 6.0 to simplify memory management in CUDA applications. It allows for automatic migration of data between the host and device. 

However, the way this feature is implemented can lead to performance degradation on certain architectures. In particular, the Pascal architecture has a hardware feature called "Page Migration Engine" that can cause extra overhead when used with `cudaMallocManaged`. This can result in slower performance compared to using `cudaMalloc`.

Here’s what you can do to improve the kernel execution time:

1. Use cudaMalloc instead of cudaMallocManaged. This will require you to manually manage the memory transfer using cudaMemcpy, but it can result in better performance.

Here is the updated code snippet where `cudaMalloc` is used instead of `cudaMallocManaged`:

```cpp
__global__ static void CUDAKernelAddOneToVector(int *data)
{
  const int x  = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
  const int y  = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;
  const int mx = gridDim.x * blockDim.x;

  data[y * mx + x] = data[y * mx + x] + 1.0f;
}

void CUDASample::AddOneToVector(std::vector<int> &in){
  int *data;
  cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void **>(&data), in.size() * sizeof(int));
  cudaMemcpy(reinterpret_cast<void*>(data),reinterpret_cast<void*>(in.data()), 
             in.size() * sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);

  dim3 blks(in.size()/(16*32),1);
  dim3 threads(32, 16);

  CUDAKernelAddOneToVector<<<blks, threads>>>(data);

  cudaDeviceSynchronize();

  cudaMemcpy(reinterpret_cast<void*>(in.data()),reinterpret_cast<void*>(data), 
             in.size() * sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost);

  cudaFree(data);
}
```

2. If you still want to use `cudaMallocManaged`, try using the `cudaMemAdvise` function to set the preferred location for the data. This will give hints to the CUDA runtime about the expected usage pattern of the memory, which can help improve performance. 

For example, if you know that the data will be mostly accessed by the device, you can set the preferred location to the device. Here's how you can do this:

```cpp
cudaMemAdvise(data, in.size() * sizeof(int), cudaMemAdviseSetPreferredLocation, cudaCpuDeviceId);
```

Remember that these are just potential solutions. The actual performance can vary depending on the specific use case and the hardware. It's always a good idea to profile your code to identify the performance bottlenecks and try different optimizations to see what works best.

