For example, given the input array `nums = [4, 3, 2, 7, 8, 2, 3, 1]`, the method should return `[2, 3]` as these are the duplicate elements in the array.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class GetDuplicatesAction extends AbstractGetDuplicatesAction {

    static final Logger LOG = LoggerFactory.getLogger(GetDuplicatesAction.class);

    public int[] findDuplicates(int[] nums) {
        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int num : nums) {
            if (!set.add(num)) {
                if (!result.contains(num)) {
                    result.add(num);
                }
            }
        }

        return result.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
    }
}
```

In the solution, we use a HashSet to efficiently store unique elements encountered while iterating through the input array. If an element is already in the set, it is considered a duplicate and added to the result list. Finally, the result list is converted back to an array and returned. This solution has a time complexity of O(n) due to the efficient lookup operations provided by the HashSet.

