Here's an example of a PHP function that calculates the difference between two dates in months, weeks, days, hours, minutes, and seconds, while also considering leap years, timezones, daylight saving time changes, and the difference in time between different timezones:

```php
function calculateDateDifference($date1, $date2) {
  // Convert the input dates to DateTime objects
  $dateTime1 = new DateTime($date1);
  $dateTime2 = new DateTime($date2);

  // Get the difference between the two dates
  $interval = $dateTime1->diff($dateTime2);

  // Calculate the total months
  $months = ($interval->y * 12) + $interval->m;

  // Calculate the total weeks
  $weeks = floor($interval->days / 7);

  // Calculate the remaining days
  $days = $interval->days % 7;

  // Calculate the total hours, minutes, and seconds
  $hours = $interval->h;
  $minutes = $interval->i;
  $seconds = $interval->s;

  // Calculate the timezone offset in seconds
  $timezoneOffset = $dateTime2->getOffset() - $dateTime1->getOffset();

  // Adjust the hours, minutes, and seconds for the timezone offset
  $adjustedHours = $hours - floor($timezoneOffset / 3600);
  $adjustedMinutes = $minutes - floor(($timezoneOffset % 3600) / 60);
  $adjustedSeconds = $seconds - ($timezoneOffset % 60);

  // Handle negative values
  if ($adjustedSeconds < 0) {
    $adjustedSeconds += 60;
    $adjustedMinutes--;
  }
  if ($adjustedMinutes < 0) {
    $adjustedMinutes += 60;
    $adjustedHours--;
  }
  if ($adjustedHours < 0) {
    $adjustedHours += 24;
    $days--;
  }

  // Return the calculated difference in an array
  return [
    'months' => $months,
    'weeks' => $weeks,
    'days' => $days,
    'hours' => $adjustedHours,
    'minutes' => $adjustedMinutes,
    'seconds' => $adjustedSeconds
  ];
}

