To merge two sorted arrays in-place with a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(1), we can use a variation of the merge sort algorithm.

The idea is to start from the end of both arrays and compare the elements. We then place the larger element at the end of the first array, and continue this process until we have merged all the elements.

Here is the Java code to merge the two sorted arrays in-place:

```java
public static void mergeArrays(int[] arr1, int[] arr2, int m, int n) {
    int i = m - 1; // Index of last element in arr1
    int j = n - 1; // Index of last element in arr2
    int k = m + n - 1; // Index of last element in merged array

    while (i >= 0 && j >= 0) {
        if (arr1[i] >= arr2[j]) {
            arr1[k] = arr1[i];
            i--;
        } else {
            arr1[k] = arr2[j];
            j--;
        }
        k--;
    }

    // If there are any remaining elements in arr2, copy them to arr1
    while (j >= 0) {
        arr1[k] = arr2[j];
        j--;
        k--;
    }
}
```

To test the code, you can use the following example:

