To delete a node from a doubly linked list at a given position without using any extra variables or loops, we can use the following approach:

1. Check if the linked list is empty. If it is, return.
2. If the position is 1:
   a. Set the head of the linked list to be the next node.
   b. If the new head is not null, set its previous pointer to null.
   c. Return.
3. Initialize a pointer variable `current` to point to the head of the linked list.
4. Declare a variable `count` and initialize it to 1.
5. Traverse the linked list using the `next` pointer of each node until either we reach the given position or the end of the list.
6. If the current position is valid (i.e., we reached the desired position):
   a. Set the `prev` node's `next` pointer to the `next` node.
   b. If the `next` node is not null, set its `prev` pointer to the `prev` node.
   c. Return.
7. If we reach the end of the list without finding the desired position, the position is invalid (out of range).

Here is the C++ code to implement the above logic:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* prev;
    Node* next;
};

void deleteNode(Node** head, int position) {
    if (*head == nullptr) {
        // Linked list is empty
        return;
    }

    if (position == 1) {
        Node* newHead = (*head)->next;
        if (newHead != nullptr) {
            newHead->prev = nullptr;
        }
        *head = newHead;
        return;
    }

    Node* current = *head;
    int count = 1;

    while (current != nullptr && count < position) {
        current = current->next;
        count++;
    }

    if (current != nullptr) {
        Node* prevNode = current->prev;
        Node* nextNode = current->next;

        prevNode->next = nextNode;

        if (nextNode != nullptr) {
            nextNode->prev = prevNode;
        }
    } else {
        // Invalid position
        std::cout << "Invalid position" << std::endl;
    }
}

void displayList(Node* head) {
    while (head != nullptr) {
        std::cout << head->data << " -> ";
        head = head->next;
    }
    std::cout << "NULL" << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    // Create the linked list
    Node* head = new Node();
    Node* node1 = new Node();
    Node* node2 = new Node();
    Node* node3 = new Node();
    Node* node4 = new Node();

    head->data = 84;
    node1->data = 13;
    node2->data = 25;
    node3->data = 48;
    node4->data = 50;

    head->prev = nullptr;
    head->next = node1;
    node1->prev = head;
    node1->next = node2;
    node2->prev = node1;
    node2->next = node3;
    node3->prev = node2;
    node3->next = node4;
    node4->prev = node3;
    node4->next = nullptr;

    int position = 3;

    std::cout << "Before deletion: ";
    displayList(head);

    deleteNode(&head, position);

    std::cout << "After deletion: ";
    displayList(head);

    // Deallocate memory used by the linked list
    delete head;
    delete node1;
    delete node2;
    delete node3;
    delete node4;

    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a doubly linked list with the given values. It then deletes the node at the specified position and displays the updated linked list.

Please note that this code assumes the given position is valid (i.e., it does not check if the position is within the range of the linked list).

Certainly, optimization is always a good approach. When deleting a node, we can directly connect the previous node to the next node, bypassing the currently visited node. Also, we need to deallocate the memory of the node to prevent memory leaks. 

Here is the optimized code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* prev;
    Node* next;
};

void deleteNode(Node** head, int position) {
    Node* current = *head;

    // For head node
    if (position == 1) {
        *head = (*head)->next;
        if (*head != nullptr) {
            (*head)->prev = nullptr;
        }
        delete current;
        return;
    }

    // Move to the desired position
    while (--position) {
        current = current->next;
        if (current == nullptr) {
            std::cout << "Position out of range" << std::endl;
            return;
        }
    }

    // Not last node
    if (current->next != nullptr) {
        current->prev->next = current->next;
        current->next->prev = current->prev;
    } else { // For last node
        current->prev->next = nullptr;  
    }

    delete current; // Prevent memory leak
}

int main() {
    // Create the linked list
    Node* head = new Node{84, nullptr, nullptr};
    head->next = new Node{13, head, nullptr};
    head->next->next = new Node{25, head->next, nullptr};
    head->next->next->next = new Node{48, head->next->next, nullptr};
    head->next->next->next->next = new Node{50, head->next->next->next, nullptr};

    int position = 3;

    deleteNode(&head, position);

    for (Node* temp = head; temp != nullptr; temp = temp->next) {
        std::cout << temp->data << ' ';
    }

    // Deallocate memory used by the linked list
    while (head != nullptr) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        delete head;
        head = next;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code correctly handles the deletion of nodes from a doubly linked list at any position and properly deallocates memory to prevent memory leaks after deletion. It also handles edge cases such as position being out of range.
